@code_type rust .rs
@comment_type // %s

@title Tangler

@s From Resolved Definitions to a Planned File

Resolver ended with two final definitions and one **root**: a definition whose
name is also the path of a complete output file.

```text
ResolvedProgram
|-- blocks[0] "hello.txt"
|   `-- "@{Greeting line}"        source line 6
|-- blocks[1] "Greeting line"
|   |-- "Good morning"            source line 20
|   `-- "from Litweb"             source line 24
`-- roots[0] -> blocks[0] -> "hello.txt"
```

The root's only line is a **whole-line reference**: after surrounding
whitespace is removed, the entire line has the form `@{Name}`. Tangler
**expands** that line by replacing it with the final lines of the selected
definition. The proposed `hello.txt` is therefore:

```text
Good morning
from Litweb
```

Each emitted text line receives one LF (`\n`), including the final line. The
complete in-memory result is:

```text
TanglePlan
|-- outputs[0]
|   |-- relative path "hello.txt"
|   |-- bytes "Good morning\nfrom Litweb\n"
|   `-- origin greeting.lit:5
`-- warnings []
```

A **planned output** contains a relative path, complete bytes, and the source
location that requested the file. It is still only a proposal: Tangler has
not opened `hello.txt` or changed the filesystem.

The public @code{plan_tangle} function accepts Parser's @code{Program} and
invokes Resolver. Once resolution succeeds, the expansion algorithm works
from the resulting @code{ResolvedProgram}. It follows final definitions and
roots; it does not collect bases, apply changes, or discover roots again.

@s Following the First Reference

The small example gives the general expansion rule in concrete form:

1. Start at `blocks[0]`, the `hello.txt` root.
2. Read its line `@{Greeting line}`.
3. Recognize the whole-line reference and look up `Greeting line`, obtaining
   `blocks[1]`.
4. Enter `blocks[1]`.
5. Emit `Good morning` and an LF, then `from Litweb` and an LF.
6. Finish `blocks[1]`, finish the root, and store the bytes in the plan.

Conceptually, step 4 is a recursive call: pause the root, expand its child,
then resume the root. A **frame** records one such paused expansion—its
definition, next line, and inherited indentation. While the example runs, the
frame stack and output grow as follows:

```text
frames: [hello.txt at line 0]
output: ""

frames: [hello.txt at end, Greeting line at line 0]
output: ""

frames: [hello.txt at end, Greeting line at line 1]
output: "Good morning\n"

frames: [hello.txt at end, Greeting line at end]
output: "Good morning\nfrom Litweb\n"

frames: []
output: "Good morning\nfrom Litweb\n"
```

The nested frames form the **active chain**. If an active definition is
referenced again, expansion would never finish; that reference is a cycle. A
definition that has already finished is no longer active and may be reused
later.

@s The Whole-Line Reference Contract

Surrounding whitespace does not prevent expansion, so both
`@{Greeting line}` and `    @{Greeting line}` are references. Text on either
side does prevent it: `prefix @{Greeting line}` and
`@{Greeting line} suffix` are copied literally.

Leading whitespace on a genuine reference becomes **accumulated indentation**.
If a line containing four spaces and `@{Body}` selects a body
containing `println!("hello");`, the emitted line begins with those four
spaces. A reference inside `Body` adds its own leading whitespace to the
amount already inherited.

That accumulated indentation belongs before nonempty child lines. A truly
empty line has no target text to indent, so it emits only its line ending. A
line containing authored spaces or tabs is different: it is nonempty target
text, and Tangler preserves both its inherited indentation and its authored
whitespace.

Tangler and Weaver share this exact recognition rule. Otherwise the woven page
could link a line as a block use while the tangled file treated the same line
as literal text. The helpers are small:

--- src/util.rs
//! Small utilities shared by Litweb's core modules.

@{Reference and whitespace helpers}
---

--- Reference and whitespace helpers
pub(crate) fn block_reference(text: &str) -> Option<&str> {
    let stripped = text.trim();
    (stripped.starts_with("@{") && stripped.ends_with('}') && stripped.len() >= 3)
        .then(|| &stripped[2..stripped.len() - 1])
}

pub(crate) fn leading_whitespace(text: &str) -> &str {
    let end = text
        .char_indices()
        .find_map(|(index, character)| (!character.is_whitespace()).then_some(index))
        .unwrap_or(text.len());
    &text[..end]
}
---

@s The Tangler Module and Its Result

The Tangler module owns expansion, framing choices, warnings, and structured
failures. It reuses @code{PlannedOutput} from the neutral Output module, which
also receives woven files.

--- src/tangler.rs
//! Clean source generation from parsed `.lit` programs.

@{Tangler imports}
@{Tangle warnings}
@{Tangle options}
@{Tangle plan}
@{Tangle plan failures}
@{Expansion error model}
@{Render one expansion error}
@{Expansion error collection}
@{Plan tangle function}
@{Expansion frames}
@{First nonblank indentation}
@{Block-separator helper}
@{Expand one root}
@{Write tangled outputs function}
---

The dependencies express those boundaries: Resolver supplies definitions and
lookup results, Parser supplies the public input and source locations, the
shared utility recognizes references, and Output owns planned files and
filesystem replacement.

--- Tangler imports
use std::fmt;
use std::path::Path;

pub use crate::output::{
    OutputAction, OutputError, OutputErrorKind, PlannedOutput, write_planned_outputs,
    write_planned_outputs_protecting,
};
use crate::parser::{Program, SourceOrigin};
use crate::resolver::{BlockLookup, ResolveErrors, ResolvedProgram, resolve};
use crate::util::{block_reference, leading_whitespace};
---

@code{TanglePlan} groups the ordered file proposals with any nonfatal
warnings. A fatal resolution or expansion failure returns no plan.

--- Tangle plan
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct TanglePlan {
    pub outputs: Vec<PlannedOutput>,
    pub warnings: Vec<TangleWarning>,
}
---

@s Recursive Meaning and an Explicit Stack

For any one root, expansion means:

1. enter the definition and emit its optional generated header;
2. visit its final lines in order;
3. when two references are adjacent, add a separator if the first child did
   not already end with a blank line;
4. expand a selected whole-line reference at its inherited indentation; and
5. otherwise copy a nonempty ordinary line with its indentation and one LF,
   or emit only the LF for an empty line.

A direct recursive Rust function would make a process-stack call for every
nested reference. Valid input can be much deeper than the process stack
allows, so Tangler stores the frames in a @code{Vec} on the heap instead.
@code{next_line} marks where a paused definition will resume.
@code{indentation} is the whitespace inherited through its reference chain.
@code{entered} prevents a header from being repeated, and
@code{previous_reference_start} supports the separator rule.

--- Expansion frames
struct ExpansionFrame {
    block: usize,
    next_line: usize,
    indentation: String,
    entered: bool,
    previous_reference_start: Option<usize>,
}
---

The outer operation owns the growing output, ordered errors, active chain, and
frame stack. Each pass through the loop enters the top frame if necessary,
finishes it if no lines remain, or processes exactly one next line.

--- Expand one root
fn expand_root(
    resolved: &ResolvedProgram,
    root: usize,
    options: &TangleOptions,
) -> (String, Vec<ExpansionError>) {
    @{Initialize root expansion}

    while !frames.is_empty() {
        @{Enter an expansion frame}
        @{Finish an expansion frame}
        @{Process the next expansion line}
    }

    (output, errors)
}
---

@s Starting a Root

One root begins with no bytes or errors. @code{active} lists the definition
identities on the current chain. The parallel @code{active_positions} vector
answers “is this definition active, and where did it enter the chain?” without
searching the whole chain. The one initial frame selects the root and inherits
no indentation.

--- Initialize root expansion
let mut output = String::new();
let mut errors = Vec::new();
let mut active = Vec::new();
let mut active_positions = vec![None; resolved.blocks.len()];
let mut first_indentations = vec![FirstIndentation::Unknown; resolved.blocks.len()];
let mut frames = vec![ExpansionFrame {
    block: root,
    next_line: 0,
    indentation: String::new(),
    entered: false,
    previous_reference_start: None,
}];
---

@s Entering and Finishing a Frame

On first entry, Tangler emits the definition's header when the selected
options and definition permit one. It then records the definition on the
active chain.

--- Enter an expansion frame
let depth = frames.len() - 1;
let block_index = frames[depth].block;
let block = resolved.block(block_index);

if !frames[depth].entered {
    if options.headers && !block.comment_string.is_empty() && !block.no_header {
        output.push_str(&frames[depth].indentation);
        if let Some(indentation) = first_nonblank_indentation(
            resolved,
            block_index,
            &mut first_indentations,
        ) {
            output.push_str(&indentation);
        }
        output.push_str(&block.comment_string.replace("%s", &block.name));
        output.push('\n');
    }
    frames[depth].entered = true;
    active_positions[block_index] = Some(active.len());
    active.push(block_index);
}
---

After its final line, the definition leaves both the frame stack and active
chain. It can then be used by another branch without being mistaken for a
cycle.

--- Finish an expansion frame
if frames[depth].next_line == block.lines.len() {
    frames.pop();
    let exited = active.pop().expect("entered frame has an active block");
    active_positions[exited] = None;
    continue;
}
---

@s Processing the Next Line

The next final line still carries the chapter in which it was authored.
Tangler first advances the current frame, then either expands a recognized
reference or emits literal text.

For a reference, the operation applies a sibling separator when needed and
asks Resolver's lookup index for the target. A missing or ambiguous name is
recorded at the use line. A target already on the active chain produces the
closed cycle. Every other target receives a new child frame whose indentation
combines the parent's inherited whitespace with the reference line's own
leading whitespace. The ordinary-line branch instead copies the text and adds
one LF. It omits inherited indentation when that text is empty, preventing a
structurally blank line from acquiring trailing spaces.

--- Process the next expansion line
let line_index = frames[depth].next_line;
let line = block.lines[line_index].clone();
let line_chapter = block.line_chapters[line_index];
frames[depth].next_line += 1;
let indentation = frames[depth].indentation.clone();
if let Some(name) = block_reference(&line.text) {
    if options.block_separators
        && frames[depth]
            .previous_reference_start
            .is_some_and(|start| !ends_with_blank_line(&output[start..]))
    {
        output.push('\n');
    }
    frames[depth].previous_reference_start = Some(output.len());
    let name = name.to_owned();
    let target = match resolved.lookup(line_chapter, &name) {
        BlockLookup::Found(target) => target,
        BlockLookup::Missing => {
            errors.push(ExpansionError {
                origin: line.origin,
                kind: ExpansionErrorKind::UndefinedBlock { name },
            });
            continue;
        }
        BlockLookup::Ambiguous(indices) => {
            errors.push(ExpansionError {
                origin: line.origin,
                kind: ExpansionErrorKind::AmbiguousBlock {
                    name,
                    candidates: indices
                        .iter()
                        .map(|index| resolved.block(*index).origin.clone())
                        .collect(),
                },
            });
            continue;
        }
    };

    if let Some(position) = active_positions[target] {
        let mut cycle = active[position..].to_vec();
        cycle.push(target);
        let first_chapter = resolved.block(cycle[0]).chapter;
        let crosses_chapters = cycle
            .iter()
            .any(|index| resolved.block(*index).chapter != first_chapter);
        let kind = if crosses_chapters {
            ExpansionErrorKind::CrossChapterReferenceCycle {
                blocks: cycle
                    .iter()
                    .map(|index| CycleBlock {
                        name: resolved.block(*index).name.clone(),
                        origin: resolved.block(*index).origin.clone(),
                    })
                    .collect(),
            }
        } else {
            ExpansionErrorKind::ReferenceCycle {
                names: cycle
                    .iter()
                    .map(|index| resolved.block(*index).name.clone())
                    .collect(),
            }
        };
        errors.push(ExpansionError {
            origin: line.origin,
            kind,
        });
        continue;
    }

    frames.push(ExpansionFrame {
        block: target,
        next_line: 0,
        indentation: indentation + leading_whitespace(&line.text),
        entered: false,
        previous_reference_start: None,
    });
} else {
    frames[depth].previous_reference_start = None;
    if !line.text.is_empty() {
        output.push_str(&indentation);
    }
    output.push_str(&line.text);
    output.push('\n');
}
---

@s Generated Headers and Separators

Tangler can add **generated framing** that was not authored as target code:
block-name comments help a reader navigate the tangled file, and one blank
separator can distinguish adjacent child definitions. This exact program makes
both additions visible:

```text
@comment_type // %s
@s Program
--- output.rs
fn main() {
    @{Body}
    @{Ending}
}
---
--- Body
println!("hello");
---
--- Ending
return;
---
```

With ordinary options, Tangler proposes:

```rust
// output.rs
fn main() {
    // Body
    println!("hello");

    // Ending
    return;
}
```

The root and both children receive headers from `// %s`. Child headers and
lines inherit the four spaces before each reference. Because `Body` and
`Ending` are adjacent references and `Body` did not end with a blank authored
line, Tangler inserts one separator.

With both kinds of framing disabled, the same program produces:

```rust
fn main() {
    println!("hello");
    return;
}
```

@code{TangleOptions} chooses the two additions independently. Both are enabled
by default.

--- Tangle options
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct TangleOptions {
    pub headers: bool,
    pub block_separators: bool,
}

impl Default for TangleOptions {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            headers: true,
            block_separators: true,
        }
    }
}
---

An empty comment pattern or a definition marked `noHeader` supplies no header.
An authored blank line at a child boundary already supplies separation.
Literal parent code ends adjacency, and Tangler adds no separator before the
first child or after the root. Disabling framing removes only generated
material; authored target text remains under Tangler's LF policy. Input CRLF
line endings are not preserved byte for byte.

The separator test needs only the bytes produced since the preceding child
began. This helper reports whether those bytes end in an empty or
whitespace-only line.

--- Block-separator helper
fn ends_with_blank_line(output: &str) -> bool {
    let Some(without_ending) = output.strip_suffix('\n') else {
        return false;
    };
    without_ending
        .rsplit('\n')
        .next()
        .is_some_and(|line| line.chars().all(char::is_whitespace))
}
---

@s Aligning a Header with Its First Visible Line

A block may begin with blank lines or with another reference. Its header is
written before either has been expanded, but it should align with the first
nonblank authored line that will eventually appear. For example, if an
indented reference selects `Wrapper`, and `Wrapper` begins with a further
two-space reference to `Leaf`, the `Wrapper` header needs both amounts of
indentation:

```text
    @{Wrapper}

--- Wrapper
  @{Leaf}
---

--- Leaf
text
---
```

The first visible line from `Wrapper` is `Leaf`'s `text`, six spaces from the
outer margin. Its generated header therefore also begins six spaces from the
margin.

@code{FirstIndentation} caches the first answer found for each definition so
repeated uses need not scan the same nested lines again. @code{Unknown} means
the definition has not been inspected, @code{Visiting} means it is on the
lookahead stack, and @code{Known} stores either its first relative indentation
or the fact that it emits no nonblank line.

The lookahead has its own explicit stack. It skips blank lines, follows
whole-line references, and combines their leading whitespace. Missing and
cyclic paths are treated as empty during lookahead because ordinary expansion
will report the real error at the use line and discard the failed plan.

--- First nonblank indentation
#[derive(Clone)]
enum FirstIndentation {
    Unknown,
    Visiting,
    Known(Option<String>),
}

struct FirstIndentationFrame {
    block: usize,
    next_line: usize,
}

fn first_nonblank_indentation(
    resolved: &ResolvedProgram,
    root: usize,
    states: &mut [FirstIndentation],
) -> Option<String> {
    if let FirstIndentation::Known(indentation) = &states[root] {
        return indentation.clone();
    }

    states[root] = FirstIndentation::Visiting;
    let mut frames = vec![FirstIndentationFrame {
        block: root,
        next_line: 0,
    }];

    while !frames.is_empty() {
        let depth = frames.len() - 1;
        let block_index = frames[depth].block;
        let block = resolved.block(block_index);
        if frames[depth].next_line == block.lines.len() {
            states[block_index] = FirstIndentation::Known(None);
            frames.pop();
            continue;
        }

        let line_index = frames[depth].next_line;
        let line = &block.lines[line_index];
        if let Some(name) = block_reference(&line.text) {
            let line_chapter = block.line_chapters[line_index];
            let BlockLookup::Found(target) = resolved.lookup(line_chapter, name) else {
                frames[depth].next_line += 1;
                continue;
            };
            match states[target].clone() {
                FirstIndentation::Unknown => {
                    states[target] = FirstIndentation::Visiting;
                    frames.push(FirstIndentationFrame {
                        block: target,
                        next_line: 0,
                    });
                }
                FirstIndentation::Visiting | FirstIndentation::Known(None) => {
                    frames[depth].next_line += 1;
                }
                FirstIndentation::Known(Some(child_indentation)) => {
                    let mut indentation = leading_whitespace(&line.text).to_owned();
                    indentation.push_str(&child_indentation);
                    states[block_index] = FirstIndentation::Known(Some(indentation));
                    frames.pop();
                }
            }
        } else if line.text.trim().is_empty() {
            frames[depth].next_line += 1;
        } else {
            states[block_index] =
                FirstIndentation::Known(Some(leading_whitespace(&line.text).to_owned()));
            frames.pop();
        }
    }

    let FirstIndentation::Known(indentation) = &states[root] else {
        unreachable!("completed lookahead has a known result");
    };
    indentation.clone()
}
---

@s Planning Every Root

@code{plan_tangle} selects ordinary framing.
@code{plan_tangle_with_options} first resolves the public @code{Program}, then
expands each resolved root in source order. In the `Greeting` example that
single loop iteration creates the path, bytes, and origin shown at the
chapter's beginning.

With several roots, output order remains root order. Tangler continues after
one root fails so independent roots produce a stable, complete error list.
If any expansion error exists, it discards every accumulated output and
returns no @code{TanglePlan}. This is an all-or-nothing in-memory plan, not a
filesystem transaction.

--- Plan tangle function
pub fn plan_tangle(program: &Program) -> Result<TanglePlan, PlanError> {
    plan_tangle_with_options(program, TangleOptions::default())
}

pub fn plan_tangle_with_options(
    program: &Program,
    options: TangleOptions,
) -> Result<TanglePlan, PlanError> {
    let resolved = resolve(program).map_err(PlanError::Resolution)?;
    let mut outputs = Vec::with_capacity(resolved.roots.len());
    let mut errors = Vec::new();

    for root in &resolved.roots {
        let (bytes, mut root_errors) = expand_root(&resolved, root.block, &options);
        errors.append(&mut root_errors);
        outputs.push(PlannedOutput {
            relative_path: root.path.clone(),
            bytes: bytes.into_bytes(),
            origin: root.origin.clone(),
        });
    }

    if !errors.is_empty() {
        return Err(PlanError::Expansion(ExpansionErrors { errors }));
    }

    let warnings = if resolved.roots.is_empty() {
        vec![TangleWarning {
            origin: program.origin.clone(),
            kind: TangleWarningKind::NoRoots,
        }]
    } else {
        Vec::new()
    };
    Ok(TanglePlan { outputs, warnings })
}
---

Some documents exist only to weave an explanation and contain no file-shaped
root. That is valid: planning returns an empty output vector and a
@code{NoRoots} warning instead of failing.

--- Tangle warnings
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct TangleWarning {
    pub origin: SourceOrigin,
    pub kind: TangleWarningKind,
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum TangleWarningKind {
    NoRoots,
}

impl fmt::Display for TangleWarning {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
        match self.kind {
            TangleWarningKind::NoRoots => write!(
                f,
                "{}: warning: no file code blocks; no code written",
                self.origin
            ),
        }
    }
}
---

@s References Authored in Book Chapters

Resolver's English/Spanish example produced this final root:

```text
ResolvedBlock "hello.txt"          base chapter: English
|-- "Hello"                        line chapter: English
`-- "@{Punctuation}"               line chapter: Spanish
```

English and Spanish each define `Punctuation`, but Spanish authored the added
reference. Tangler passes that line's chapter to Resolver's local-first lookup,
so the reference selects Spanish's definition. The authored output is:

```text
Hello
¡
```

The base chapter identifies the root definition; the chapter stored beside
each final line identifies where that line's references should look first.
Cycle detection still uses stable definition identities, so equal local names
in different chapters are not confused.

@s Planning Failures

Planning can fail at two different stages. A resolution failure means the
bases, changes, roots, or paths were invalid before expansion. An expansion
failure means a reachable root contained a missing, ambiguous, or cyclic
reference. The public error keeps that distinction.

--- Tangle plan failures
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum PlanError {
    Resolution(ResolveErrors),
    Expansion(ExpansionErrors),
}

impl fmt::Display for PlanError {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
        match self {
            Self::Resolution(errors) => write!(f, "{errors}"),
            Self::Expansion(errors) => write!(f, "{errors}"),
        }
    }
}

impl std::error::Error for PlanError {}
---

Every expansion error retains the origin of the use line. Missing names,
ambiguous names, same-chapter cycles, and cross-chapter cycles carry the
details a caller needs to inspect or display the problem.

--- Expansion error model
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct ExpansionError {
    pub origin: SourceOrigin,
    pub kind: ExpansionErrorKind,
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum ExpansionErrorKind {
    UndefinedBlock {
        name: String,
    },
    AmbiguousBlock {
        name: String,
        candidates: Vec<SourceOrigin>,
    },
    ReferenceCycle {
        names: Vec<String>,
    },
    CrossChapterReferenceCycle {
        blocks: Vec<CycleBlock>,
    },
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct CycleBlock {
    pub name: String,
    pub origin: SourceOrigin,
}
---

The display boundary turns one structured error into a source-located
sentence. Cross-chapter cycles include the origins of their definitions so
repeated local names remain distinguishable.

--- Render one expansion error
impl fmt::Display for ExpansionError {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
        write!(f, "{}: error: ", self.origin)?;
        match &self.kind {
            ExpansionErrorKind::UndefinedBlock { name } => {
                write!(f, "code block {{{name}}} is not defined")
            }
            ExpansionErrorKind::AmbiguousBlock { name, candidates } => {
                write!(
                    f,
                    "code block {{{name}}} is ambiguous; definitions are at {}",
                    candidates
                        .iter()
                        .map(ToString::to_string)
                        .collect::<Vec<_>>()
                        .join(", ")
                )
            }
            ExpansionErrorKind::ReferenceCycle { names } => {
                write!(f, "code block reference cycle: {}", names.join(" -> "))
            }
            ExpansionErrorKind::CrossChapterReferenceCycle { blocks } => write!(
                f,
                "code block reference cycle: {}",
                blocks
                    .iter()
                    .map(|block| format!("{} at {}", block.name, block.origin))
                    .collect::<Vec<_>>()
                    .join(" -> ")
            ),
        }
    }
}

impl std::error::Error for ExpansionError {}
---

The aggregate preserves root-order discovery for library callers. Its short
display reports the first problem and the number still available in the
collection.

--- Expansion error collection
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct ExpansionErrors {
    errors: Vec<ExpansionError>,
}

impl ExpansionErrors {
    pub fn as_slice(&self) -> &[ExpansionError] {
        &self.errors
    }

    pub fn into_vec(self) -> Vec<ExpansionError> {
        self.errors
    }
}

impl fmt::Display for ExpansionErrors {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
        if let Some(first) = self.errors.first() {
            write!(f, "{first}")?;
            if self.errors.len() > 1 {
                write!(f, " (and {} more expansion errors)", self.errors.len() - 1)?;
            }
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}

impl std::error::Error for ExpansionErrors {}
---

@s Handing the Completed Plan to Output

The non-mutating planning functions stop with all paths and bytes in memory.
For callers that want to write only one input's tangled files,
@code{write_outputs} passes the completed plan to Output's neutral writer.
Main uses Output's broader multi-input entry point when it combines tangled
and woven plans for a book.

--- Write tangled outputs function
pub fn write_outputs(
    plan: &TanglePlan,
    output_directory: &Path,
    input: &Path,
) -> Result<(), OutputError> {
    write_planned_outputs(&plan.outputs, output_directory, input)
}
---

For `Greeting`, the handoff is still the one `hello.txt` proposal shown at the
beginning. Tangler has followed the reference, applied the LF policy, and
finished every root without an expansion error. Output can now preflight the
complete proposal before it creates or replaces any file.
